//? Title: MobileNet v3: Hard-Swish, Channel Gates and Searched Blocks
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
MobileNet v3 combines searched bottleneck tables with hard-swish and squeeze/excitation.
We plot the activation replacements, check channel-gate values and derivatives, verify both
released sizes, then explicitly fit Large and Small CIFAR models with tensor Adam.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.4_efficient_and_mobile_architectures/mobilenet_v3.cpp`.

> The method

Hard sigmoid and hard-swish replace smooth functions with simple piecewise polynomials:

$$h(x)=\operatorname{ReLU6}(x+3)/6,\qquad \operatorname{hswish}(x)=x h(x).$$
\alttext(The gate is clipped linear; multiplying by x produces a piecewise quadratic activation.)

Hard-swish is zero at x<=-3, identity at x>=3 and x(x+3)/6 inside; its interior derivative
x/3+.5 can be negative. Hinges use the core ReLU6 derivative convention, so the independent
slope check excludes exactly ±3. The plots compare both replacements with sigmoid/swish.
Squeeze/excitation pools each image spatially, reduces channels with biased 1×1/ReLU, expands
with biased 1×1/hard-sigmoid, then broadcasts gates across the original feature map. A controlled
zero expansion kernel isolates exact gate values/input derivatives; general gates also depend on x.

Large/Small retain the exact 15/11 released searched blocks, with 3×3/5×5 depthwise filters,
ReLU or hard-swish, optional SE and linear projections. Equal-width stride-one sums remain ungated.
The efficient final spatial 1×1 expands to six times the last bottleneck width; pooling precedes
a biased 1280/1024-unit hard-swish head, dropout .2 and classifier. Full learned counts are
5483032/2542856, with 24400/12112 moving scalars and 216589760/56510400 counted MACs at 224.
These counts include pooled SE filters and both dense matrices but omit elementwise channel scaling.
Full-model gradient probes shift BN betas/SE biases and separately check 15/11 redundant projection
BN shifts. Both probes retain complete widths/tables at 64-pixel synthetic resolution, without dropout.

CIFAR Large/Small retain 4214842/1528106 learned scalars; only the class count and initial stride change.
All model computations and fitting use the tensor library. BN learns scale/shift with epsilon .001;
training uses batch moments and eager moving updates use unbiased variance. He-uniform fan-in
initialization is an adaptation of the original release. Images stay at original 32×32 resolution;
only the first stride changes to one, retaining every later block/downsampling. Fits use 128 fitting,
64 validation and 64 official-test images, 10 batch-eight epochs of Adam(.001), explicit kernel
L2=.0001/2 and independent padded crops/flips. Validation selects paired weights/moving moments
before test evaluation. These tiny CPU fits differ from both the larger source runs and published
ImageNet schedules. MAC counts are arithmetic budgets from measured output shapes, not latency.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/mobilenet_v3_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/mobilenet_v3_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/mobilenet_v3_concept.tikz > build/mobilenet_v3_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/mobilenet_v3_concept.svg build/mobilenet_v3_concept.dvi > build/mobilenet_v3_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
